
Bit Manipulation in C – Complete Concept & Practice

This document covers the fundamentals of bit manipulation in C, common bitwise
operations, applications, and a set of solved problems ranging from easy to hard.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
1.  INTRODUCTION
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
What:
    Bit manipulation is the act of algorithmically manipulating individual bits
    of a number. In C, it uses bitwise operators to perform operations directly
    on binary representations.

Why:
    • Faster than arithmetic operations (CPUs handle bits natively)
    • Saves memory (pack multiple flags into a single integer)
    • Essential in low‑level programming, embedded systems, cryptography, and
      competitive programming

Where:
    • Setting/clearing flags in registers
    • Compression and encryption
    • Efficient mathematical checks (odd/even, power of two, etc.)
    • Graph algorithms (bitmask DP)
    • Network protocols (bit fields)

How:
    Using the six bitwise operators: &, |, ^, ~, <<, >>

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
2.  BITWISE OPERATORS
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Operator  Symbol  Meaning
AND       &       Result bit = 1 if both bits are 1, else 0
OR        |       Result bit = 1 if at least one bit is 1
XOR       ^       Result bit = 1 if bits are different
NOT       ~       Flips all bits (unary)
Left Shift   <<   Shifts bits left, fills LSBs with 0 (multiply by 2^n)
Right Shift  >>   Shifts bits right; for unsigned, fills with 0;
                  for signed, behaviour is implementation‑defined
                  (usually arithmetic shift on most compilers – fills with sign bit)

Examples (8‑bit for clarity):
    a = 5 (0000 0101), b = 9 (0000 1001)
    a & b  → 0000 0001 = 1
    a | b  → 0000 1101 = 13
    a ^ b  → 0000 1100 = 12
    ~a     → 1111 1010 (signed interpretation = -6 in two’s complement)
    a << 1 → 0000 1010 = 10
    b >> 2 → 0000 0010 = 2

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
3.  COMMON BIT OPERATIONS
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Let n be an integer, k the bit position (0‑based from LSB).

Check if k‑th bit is set:      n & (1 << k)  ≠ 0
Set k‑th bit:                  n = n | (1 << k)
Clear k‑th bit:                n = n & ~(1 << k)
Toggle k‑th bit:               n = n ^ (1 << k)

Least significant set bit (LSB):  n & -n   (gives value of lowest set bit)
Remove LSB:                       n & (n-1)  (clears the lowest set bit)
Count set bits:                   repeatedly clear LSB until 0
Get bit at position:              (n >> k) & 1

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
4.  BIT MASKS
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
A mask is a sequence of bits used to select or modify specific bits.

Examples:
    Mask for lower 4 bits: 0x0F  (binary 0000 1111)
    Extract lower 4 bits:  num & 0x0F
    Set lower 4 bits:      num | 0x0F
    Clear lower 4 bits:    num & ~0x0F
    Toggle lower 4 bits:   num ^ 0x0F

Creating masks:
    Mask with n least significant bits set:  (1 << n) - 1
    Mask with k‑th bit set:                 1 << k

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
5.  SHIFT OPERATORS IN DETAIL
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Left Shift (<<): Always fills right side with 0.
    Equivalent to multiplying by 2^n (if no overflow).

Right Shift (>>):
    • For unsigned types: logical shift (fill with 0).
    • For signed types: may be arithmetic (fill with sign bit) or logical.
      GCC uses arithmetic shift for signed integers.
    Safe practice: use unsigned when bit manipulation is intended.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
6.  BIT FIELDS
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Bit fields allow you to pack multiple small members into a single integer.
    struct {
        unsigned int flag1 : 1;
        unsigned int flag2 : 1;
        unsigned int mode  : 3;
    } status;
Size of status is typically 4 bytes, but the compiler packs the bits.
Limitations: non‑portable, no address‑of, subject to endianness.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
7.  ENDIANNESS AND BIT MANIPULATION
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Endianness affects byte order, but bitwise operations work on values, not on
memory layout. However, when accessing individual bytes of an integer via char*
pointers, endianness matters. Usually, bit manipulations are endian‑agnostic.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
8.  APPLICATIONS AND TRICKS
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
• Check if n is power of two:          n && !(n & (n-1))
• Check if n is even:                  (n & 1) == 0
• Swap two numbers without temp:       a ^= b; b ^= a; a ^= b;
• Multiply/divide by 2:                n << 1, n >> 1
• Compute 2^n:                         1 << n
• Set all bits to 1 in a given width:  (1 << n) - 1
• Turn off rightmost 1:                n & (n-1)
• Isolate rightmost 1:                 n & -n
• Generate all subsets (bitmask iteration): for(int mask=0; mask < (1<<n); mask++)

